WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.799
Battered by critics upon the release of its
first season in 2017, Marvel's Iron Fist needed

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:10.040
some serious image rehabilitation going into
its sophomore outing.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.881
But the show's reception and quality actually
has started to turn around - and it's even

00:00:13.881 --> 00:00:16.760
set a Rotten Tomatoes record in the process.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.449
According to a breakdown of data by the review
aggregating site, Iron Fist has set a record

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:26.260
for the biggest jump in quality between the
first and second seasons of a TV series that

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.280
Rotten Tomatoes has ever measured.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:32.890
But just because Iron Fist has clearly improved
doesn't mean the show is suddenly great now.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:37.640
According to the second season's breakdown
on the website, only 50% of critics across

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.870
30 reviews deemed the series as being worth
another look.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:45.230
On an A-to-F scale, that's still a failing
grade - though it's definitely a big improvement

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.960
over the first season's 19% score.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.699
When Iron Fist's first season was released
in 2017, critics derided the show as being

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:56.489
one of the single worst properties Marvel
Television had yet produced.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:01.350
It was a feat which to this date has been
exceeded only by ABC's Inhumans series, which

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.120
was released the same year to a 12% Rotten
Tomatoes score.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.440
Despite the major second season bump from
critics, it's hard to characterize Iron Fist

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.630
as being a must-see-series yet.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:16.010
The finding is mostly encouraging for viewers
who are already fans of the show, with the

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.830
second season having a little more of what
people like, and a little less of what they

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:20.830
don't.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:25.610
Unlike Hannibal or Breaking Bad, series which
started good and then got even better, Iron

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.520
Fist started off terribly, and is now more-or-less
"okay".

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.130
Even the more negative reviews for the series
are now characterizing the show as being "fine",

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.880
"mildly entertaining", or "merely boring."

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.350
In other words, Iron Fist is still not exactly
appointment viewing - but it probably won't

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.470
ruin your day to see an episode anymore.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:47.870
Partially explaining the turnaround in quality
is the fact that Iron Fist's second season

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.630
was written under the guidance of a new showrunner:
Raven Metzner.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:56.700
As a screenwriter and producer, Metzner previously
worked on the TV series Sleepy Hollow, as

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.240
well as the shows Heroes Reborn, Falling Skies,
and Daybreak.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.600
He also wrote the screenplay for the movie
Elektra - but that was a long time ago, so

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.020
we'll try and ignore that.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:10.680
Metzner came on board Iron Fist as a replacement
for season one's showrunner, Scott Buck, who'd

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.960
earned himself a questionable reputation as
a showrunner after overseeing the Showtime

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.860
serial killer series Dexter as it fell from
prestige drama into self-parody.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.660
He was also the showrunner for Marvel's Inhumans,
which was quietly cancelled after eight profoundly

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.090
bad episodes.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.480
Alongside the behind-the-scenes personnel
shifts, Iron Fist's second season also saw

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.200
marked improvements to its cinematography
and fight choreography, which are generally

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.810
regarded as being pretty important ingredients
for a martial arts superhero show.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:44.240
The new season also included an increased
focus on the characters Misty Knight and Colleen

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.810
Wing, better known in Marvel Comics as the
Daughters of the Dragon.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:52.400
Season two sees the fighters spend some time
teaming up, taking on the bad guys to enjoyable

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.400
effect.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.530
Misty also gets a spiffy new robotic arm,
while Colleen earns herself some impressive

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:58.530
new powers.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:03.651
In short, while Iron Fist is still the lowest-rated
series in the Marvel-Netflix collection, it's

00:03:03.651 --> 00:03:07.990
significantly closed the gap in quality between
itself and the other five shows.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:11.960
Unfortunately, Iron Fist's second season still
seems to struggle with the same issues that

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.780
affect all of the shows in Marvel's Netflix
universe.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:19.210
Whether it's Daredevil, Jessica Jones, Luke
Cage or The Punisher, every one of Marvel's

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:23.380
streaming series has been critiqued to some
extent - and Iron Fist is no exception.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:28.800
It still suffers from problematic pacing,
some aimless subplots, and occasionally subpar

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.620
production values, but the season as a whole
is a big step in the right direction.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:37.670
Now that Iron Fist is watchable, maybe it's
time to tweak and retool all the series, and

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.469
finally unlock the full potential of this
corner of the Marvel Universe.

